Give the echo canceller room for an echo that comes earlier

When the sound server misses its deadlines (PipeWire on a busy computer) it can leave out
some of what an application played or recorded around the missed cycles, and the echo then
reaches the microphone one or more cycles earlier, in samples, than the echo canceller's
alignment: about 10 ms a cycle at a quantum of 480 or 512, 21 ms at 1024. AEC3 places the
echo 2 ms (delay_headroom_samples 32 at 16 kHz) after the start of its linear filter, so an
echo even a few ms earlier was out of the filter's reach, and the other person's voice went
back to them for a whole turn until the delay estimator found the echo again, a second or
more later. An echo that comes later (silence played on an underflow) stays inside the
filter.

The processing now asks AEC3 for 512 samples (8 blocks, 32 ms) of headroom and makes the
refined and coarse filters, and their lengths for the first second, 8 blocks longer, so that
they reach as far after the echo as before; the same for the multichannel configuration.

Measured in the call lab (tools/call-lab/mic.sh echo, ECHO_STALL stopping the session's
PipeWire now and then, the echo's move timed in the samples each copy wrote and read),
without the patch, then with 16, 32 and 48 ms of headroom:
- moves up to 16 ms earlier followed by a turn let through: 30 in 68, 2 in 66, 3 in 56,
  9 in 63; moves of 16 to 32 ms: 26 in 79, 15 in 78, 4 in 76, 11 in 66; over 32 ms: 13 in
  53, 9 in 53, 8 in 46, 10 in 51;
- PipeWire's quantum left free (256 samples there, as on a desktop), stalls of 20 and 40 ms:
  42 turns let through in 228, 15 in 231, 7 in 227, 29 in 225;
- the quantum fixed at 480, stalls of 40 and 80 ms: 19 in 128, 14 in 130, 6 in 128, 13 in
  130;
- without stalls: none in 363, 2 in 193 (the first two turns of one call), none in 95, 4 in
  98. With 32 ms the first turn of a call is cancelled less at first (the other's voice sent
  on 57 dB under one's own at the median, 76 dB without the patch; at worst 37 dB, 33 dB
  without), the longer filters converging more slowly; the turns after it as before (71 dB
  at the median, 73 without). 48 ms let turns through even without stalls.
- WebRTC's audio threads used 2.96 % of a core with 32 ms, 2.88 % without (the same within
  the runs' spread).

